WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
oily skin owners you know how it is

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:03.600
your face is a mirrorball

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.120
everything you touch gets oily

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.400
makeup slides around

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.160
here are my top skin care&nbsp;
and makeup tips for oily skin

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.360
it's michelle of lab muffin beauty science&nbsp;
chemistry phd cosmetic chemist and shiny person

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.040
i live in australia which gets hot&nbsp;
and humid and i'm a youtuber who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.480
has to film in front of very hot lights so&nbsp;
i'm pretty experienced at dealing with oil

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.560
here's what i found works

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.440
tip one the first thing you want to&nbsp;
check is is your skin actually oily

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.320
oily skin is genetic but sometimes the wrong&nbsp;
skincare routine can irritate your skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.720
and that can make oily or even dry&nbsp;
skin leak out a whole bunch of oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.400
so it might look like your skin&nbsp;
is oily even if it's not oily

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.680
skin can also be dehydrated&nbsp;
which means it's lacking water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.480
even if there's too much oil this leads&nbsp;
to a pretty common dry but oily situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.600
where your skin feels tight and dry and&nbsp;
cracked but it's also covered in oil

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.920
a lot of people who think they have dry skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:07.120
actually have dehydrated skin unfortunately my&nbsp;
skin is truly oily technically oily and normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.040
since my cheeks are more towards normal&nbsp;
and having skin that's prone to becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:16.960
dehydrated combined with a bad skincare&nbsp;
routine made my oily skin even oilier

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.560
so the first thing to do if you're&nbsp;
frustrated with too much oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.360
is to make sure that you're not accidentally&nbsp;
making the whole situation way worse

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.040
if you want a really detailed guide to&nbsp;
doing a skin evaluation and a rundown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.760
of the different skin types and conditions&nbsp;
i've done one in my guide to basic skin care

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.080
so let's say you've confirmed that your&nbsp;
skin is actually oily what can you do

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.440
tip number two wash your face gently it's&nbsp;
very tempting to wash with harsh cleansers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:49.280
to cut through the oil and try to dry it&nbsp;
out don't do this this is a trap harsh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.160
cleansers will strip away both oil and&nbsp;
your natural moisturizing factor or nmf

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.360
this is a collection of chemicals&nbsp;
that are naturally in your skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:03.120
they hold onto water and keep your skin hydrated&nbsp;
they're your skin's natural humectant moisturizer

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.400
harsh surfactants can also warp&nbsp;
the shape of proteins in your skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.840
leading to loss of water again dehydrated skin

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.440
now your skin has evolved to be&nbsp;
very responsive to its water content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.320
when your skin's dehydrated it's triggered to&nbsp;
produce more oil to keep your skin conditioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.040
which means more of that whole&nbsp;
mirrorball situation it's a vicious cycle

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.320
so gentle cleansing is the way to break out&nbsp;
of this if your skin feels tight and dry after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.240
washing you are probably overdoing it it should&nbsp;
feel soft and plump and if you're used to bone dry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:39.760
clean skin after washing your face gentle washing&nbsp;
might actually feel like your face isn't clean

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.280
it does take some getting used to but gentle&nbsp;
cleansing was the number one thing that helped me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.840
and a lot of other people with oily skin

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:52.320
there are a lot of things that go into finding&nbsp;
a gentle cleanser but the easiest thing to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.560
is to avoid sodium lauryl sulfate in&nbsp;
the top ingredients of your cleanser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.120
and to avoid soaps i have listed some of&nbsp;
the common ones here their molecules are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.160
quite slim so they insert themselves into your&nbsp;
skin easily staying there and irritating it

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.640
and related to this is tip&nbsp;
three try washing your face less

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.600
even gentle cleansers can cause a bit of&nbsp;
this dehydration then oil production effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.000
which is why i think it's worth trying&nbsp;
just cleansing with cleanser once a day

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.680
i personally cleanse properly at night&nbsp;
and in the morning i just splash my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.480
face with water and pat it dry with a towel&nbsp;
before doing the rest of my morning routine

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.800
skincare products don't just sit on your face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.840
in one big film that blocks&nbsp;
everything else from entering

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.640
i think we tend to think of skin like&nbsp;
glass but it is more like a burger bun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.480
stuff sinks in and you can't really&nbsp;
get everything off without also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.000
removing a lot of the bun&nbsp;
or your skin's components

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.600
i've found that water in the morning&nbsp;
is enough for me and cutting down my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:53.760
skin's exposure to cleansers&nbsp;
has helped with the oil a lot

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.640
my oil ends up coming back slower throughout&nbsp;
the day instead of just in one big flood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:05.680
plus the uncomfortable tight feeling and greasy&nbsp;
dehydrated but oily situation has died down a lot

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.800
instead of having tight skin with&nbsp;
oil on top which was kind of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.080
greasy flaky plastic that had&nbsp;
been out in the sun for too long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:20.720
the oil sort of sinks in a bit more easily and&nbsp;
it's more nice and dewy rather than just greasy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.320
and a lot of people with oily skin&nbsp;
have found that this works for them too

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.400
i do sometimes cleanse a second time in the&nbsp;
middle of the day if i've been wearing a mask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.600
or if i want to take off my makeup early when i&nbsp;
do that i use bioderma micellar water which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.240
super gentle and designed for sensitive skin&nbsp;
it doesn't use proper cleansing surfactants

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:41.120
i've listed a few gentle cleansers&nbsp;
that i like in the description&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:46.160
but remember everyone's skin is different&nbsp;
so it's important to work out what your skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.880
likes and it's possible that your skin&nbsp;
does prefer to be cleansed twice a day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.560
but i think it's worth experimenting&nbsp;
with this if you haven't already

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:59.120
tip number four sort out your moisturizing&nbsp;
routine it seems to make sense that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.520
your skin's oily it doesn't need any more&nbsp;
moisturizer since it produces way too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.800
of its own already but again just like&nbsp;
the cleansing thing this can be a trap

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:14.000
your skin's natural oil isn't actually that&nbsp;
good at moisturizing there are three main ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.480
of moisturizing your skin there's humectants&nbsp;
which grab onto water and keep it in the skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.640
occlusives seal the water in&nbsp;
and emollients soften your skin

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:27.280
your natural sebum doesn't do the first&nbsp;
two very well so it just softens your skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.680
it doesn't actually help with water retention

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.800
if your skin is hydrated and oily then you might&nbsp;
not need a moisturizer you have enough of both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:40.720
water and oil but if your skin is dehydrated and&nbsp;
oily which is the case for a lot of people like me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:45.520
then having a hydrating moisturizer&nbsp;
that has humectants and occlusives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:49.600
can keep your skin's water content&nbsp;
up and that helps it control the oil

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:53.920
i like using lightweight products like&nbsp;
toners with lots of humectants during the day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:58.880
and less occlusives because they can get a&nbsp;
bit greasy and shiny i try to use hydrating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:03.680
sunscreens i also use a moisturizer at&nbsp;
night sometimes i layer an occlusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:09.120
product over humectant for extra hydration&nbsp;
and this has all made a massive difference

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:12.560
tip number five salicylic acid is great for pores

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:18.560
salicylic acid commonly known as beta hydroxy&nbsp;
acid is a chemical exfoliant and antibacterial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:23.520
skin care ingredient it gets recommended for&nbsp;
oily skin a lot because it dissolves in oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:28.640
so in theory it's more likely to be able to&nbsp;
penetrate into blackheads and clogged pores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:32.800
and exfoliate inside them which&nbsp;
means less breakouts and blackheads

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.480
now i haven't seen any actual&nbsp;
studies on whether salicylic acid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:41.760
is better at this than other exfoliants but it&nbsp;
does sound pretty reasonable i've personally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:47.360
found that my pores look a lot less noticeable&nbsp;
when i've been consistently using salicylic acid

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.880
my favorite is paula's choice 2% bha liquid but&nbsp;
there are lots of different options and if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:58.080
want to find out more about exfoliation in general&nbsp;
i have a free guide that i've linked down below

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:03.760
tip 6 is to try some oil reducing skin care&nbsp;
unfortunately there aren't that many skincare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:08.320
ingredients that have been found to reduce oil&nbsp;
on your face and for the ones where we do have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:14.000
some evidence there's usually only a single&nbsp;
study so it isn't very reliable and they might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:19.120
not actually work but if you want to try some&nbsp;
anyway here's the stuff with the most evidence

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.160
there's one study where a two percent niacinamide&nbsp;
serum reduced the amount of oil in people's faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:28.320
niacinamide is a pretty good option because&nbsp;
it does a lot of other stuff as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:33.360
another study found that a plant extract of&nbsp;
sore palmetto in a cream reduced oiliness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.640
some vitamin a derivatives like retinol and&nbsp;
tretinoin might be able to reshape the ducts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:44.480
that your sebum flows out from and that can mean&nbsp;
it cuts down on the oil pooling on your face clay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.520
is also good at soaking up oil but you do need&nbsp;
to use it regularly to get the benefits because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:55.520
it is pretty temporary although not super&nbsp;
temporary usually a clay mask twice a week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:01.040
works pretty well tip number seven is to get&nbsp;
the right translucent powder translucent powder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:05.840
is one of the most essential products in my&nbsp;
routine for soaking up oil these powders don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.880
look too obvious on your face so it's really&nbsp;
good if you don't like to wear obvious makeup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:15.440
and you can just use this over your sunscreen as&nbsp;
well the ingredient that i found works best for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:20.560
is starch so i'm always looking for starch high&nbsp;
up in the ingredients list for any translucent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:25.680
powder i've also tried using cornstarch from the&nbsp;
grocery store which actually works reasonably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:31.440
well although a proper product is a lot easier&nbsp;
to use there are also some powders that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.080
clay which tends to be pretty good at soaking up&nbsp;
oil too one thing you can try is commonly called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:40.880
the wayne goss method where you put a layer of&nbsp;
powder under your foundation so it soaks up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:45.760
oil before it gets to the surface and even before&nbsp;
it gets to your foundation so that means it won't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:50.400
slide around as much i use a big fluffy brush&nbsp;
to try to concentrate the powder on my t-zone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:56.160
which is the forehead and the nose and also these&nbsp;
bits around my nose which tend to get shiny first

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:02.560
tip number eight is to try dewy makeup it has&nbsp;
been so ingrained into my head over the years that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:07.360
matte is the way to go with makeup for&nbsp;
oily skin just to beat the mirror ball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:11.680
into submission but i've actually found&nbsp;
that dewy makeup works a lot better for me

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:15.920
when i do get oily the contrast&nbsp;
between the oily and the non-oily bits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:19.520
isn't quite as obvious so i look&nbsp;
more healthy rather than patchy

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:23.120
i usually add highlighter on the tops&nbsp;
of my cheeks and the tip of my nose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:27.920
and a little bit of my forehead as well then i&nbsp;
put starch powder lightly on top and keep topping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:32.400
up that starch powder as needed throughout&nbsp;
the day and that seems to work pretty well

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:37.040
dewy makeup is also a bit easier to blend back in&nbsp;
with your fingers throughout the day and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:42.400
related to the moisturizing tip try hydrating&nbsp;
your skin underneath before you put makeup on

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.640
for example you can try a hydrating toner&nbsp;
hydrating your skin makes it plumper and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:50.080
less textured it can smooth out&nbsp;
fine lines and reduce flaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:54.960
and that means the pigment doesn't have as many&nbsp;
road bumps on your face that can make a bunch up

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:59.920
there are a lot of silicone-based makeup products&nbsp;
and primers that are meant to absorb oil and fill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:05.280
your pores but i've actually found that these tend&nbsp;
to make me even greasier they're good for about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:10.960
half an hour until the oil soaks into them and&nbsp;
then i start looking like i'm made of melting wax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:15.680
it is not a good look but silicones do&nbsp;
work for a lot of oily skinned people

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:20.480
on the topic of makeup tip 9&nbsp;
try using less pigmented makeup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:25.680
this is not going to be possible or practical&nbsp;
for a lot of people but it is something to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:30.480
if you haven't already - using base products&nbsp;
like foundation that have less coverage

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:32.880
basically the less pigment you have on your skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.600
the less stuff there is that&nbsp;
can move around and bunch up

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:39.440
for example you can try using a sheer&nbsp;
foundation all over and then a heavier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:43.120
foundation or just concealer on the&nbsp;
bits that need a bit more coverage

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:48.720
i love and i'm currently using trinny london bff&nbsp;
serum de-stress it's a really sheer foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:53.920
that sort of just makes your skin look really&nbsp;
glowy but still natural then after that i use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:58.640
my fingers to pat in the ordinary's concealer&nbsp;
on the bits that i don't think i covered enough

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:02.720
so in total there's just less pigment&nbsp;
on my face which means the patchiness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:08.320
and bunched up bits aren't as obvious when it&nbsp;
happens because we know it is going to happen

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:11.600
you can also try using a product&nbsp;
like neon's photography fluid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:15.680
this has light reflecting particles that&nbsp;
bounce light around and sort of give the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:19.600
illusion of radiance and smoothness&nbsp;
without actually having skin colored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:24.960
pigments i found that if i use this before&nbsp;
foundation i don't need as much coverage

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:29.760
and on to my final tip tip number 10&nbsp;
look into medical treatments there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:33.120
are a few medical treatments&nbsp;
that can reduce oil production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:37.840
too many androgens which are male sex&nbsp;
hormones can cause excess oil production

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:42.800
so medications that reduce them like some&nbsp;
oral contraceptive pills and spironolactone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:48.160
can also reduce oil production isotretinoin&nbsp;
which is better known as accutane will also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:53.840
reduce oil most of the time these aren't really&nbsp;
used for treating oily skin they're used for acne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:58.080
but reducing oil is part of how they&nbsp;
work if you're interested in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:01.520
obviously speak to your doctor and&nbsp;
see if they're suitable for you

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:05.520
there are also some newer treatments that&nbsp;
are promising for reducing oil like botox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:09.680
and some laser and light treatments&nbsp;
but they're pretty expensive and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:13.440
evidence behind their effectiveness&nbsp;
just isn't super convincing yet

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:15.760
let me know in the comments if&nbsp;
there's a tip i didn't cover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:18.880
if these tips worked for you like&nbsp;
subscribe follow me on tiktok and&nbsp;&nbsp;

